# Environment files hold real credentials, so they stay
# out of version control. .env.example is committed
# instead, and shows which variables are needed without
# revealing any of their values.
.env

# Compiled binaries.
e-commerce-backend
*.exe

# Test binaries and coverage output.
*.test
*.out

# Editor and operating system clutter.
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Local database files, in case a cluster is ever created
# inside the project directory.
*.log
pgdata/

# The folder the product photographs are read from.
#
# The photographs are kept. They are the shop's own
# catalogue images, served out of this folder at
# /shop/img/, and a clone without them is a storefront
# whose every listing falls back to a drawn tile: the
# scan attaches nothing, because there is nothing there
# to attach.
#
# The rules are by extension rather than by folder, and
# that is not fussiness. The package that reads the folder
# lives in the folder: pictures.go, classify.go and the
# tests are project code. A rule ignoring the whole
# directory would leave the scanner out of every commit
# while the build carried on working from the copy on
# disk, so the omission would only surface on a fresh
# clone, as a package that does not exist.
#
# The picture extensions are the list from imageExtensions
# in pictures.go, so the two cannot drift into the state
# where the server serves a file type that version control
# does not keep. SVG is left out because the scanner
# refuses it, for the reason given there. Anything else
# dropped in the folder -- a screenshot, a stray .DS_Store
# -- is still ignored.
pictures/*
!pictures/README.md
!pictures/*.go
!pictures/*.jpg
!pictures/*.jpeg
!pictures/*.png
!pictures/*.webp
!pictures/*.gif
!pictures/*.avif

# Claude Code's local state for this project: session data
# and machine-specific settings. None of it is part of the
# application, and some of it is a transcript.
.claude/
